  "title": "The flitter floats",
  "question": "Which of these reads as a bee or a butterfly working the flowers, rather than a frog?",
  "context_md": "The flitter could only ever be thrown into the air: once airborne it read nothing, decided nothing and steered nothing, so the wire that points it at a flower was switched off for a third to a half of its life. It can now hold itself up, turn while it is up there, and set itself down on the flower it came for -- and it only does any of that when it can actually see one, so with nothing in view it is the same hopper it was.\n\n**Both panes are the same bed, the same seed and the same 400 frames, from a fixed camera.** One is the animal as it ships today, one is the float. Judge the movement -- a single frame of the two looks almost the same.\n\n**What I already measured, so you do not have to look for it.** Over 120,000 frames on your played bed, seeds 1-3: the float drinks from 76 / 8 / 5 flowers against the hopper's 68 / 14 / 4, which is inside the spread between seeds rather than clear of it, and on both arms the colony still dies out. The float spends about 30% of everything the colony eats on staying up. What did move a long way is dying in mid-air: an animal that came down on water used to hang there until it starved -- 57-87% of every death -- and it now stands on the water instead.\n\n**The counters below come from a different harness than the pictures**, and the two do not reproduce each other's animal, so the flitter you are watching is this run's own, not the one those numbers describe.",
